WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.000
So let's get started in the qualitative
processing phase.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.000
Okay, so welcome back to the desk.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:17.680
We're going to continue now with our
second step for qualitative processing.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:23.820
And actually this is our second and final
step in the qualitative processing phase.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.700
So let's get stuck in.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.280
So just as a reminder,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.000
In the first stage of our qualitative
analysis processing,

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:45.000
we engaged in the identification of a
particular company's key performance
indicators or KPIs

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:55.000
and what the management operating plan,
the MOP, of that company was through
inference of those KPIs.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:01.000
Whenever you're going through the
qualitative processing in any stock,

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:07.000
you need to actually layer additional
qualitative work on top of that.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:12.000
And this breaks down into five main areas.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
So we're going to be looking in this video
at earnings call transcripts.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.000
We're going to be looking at management
track record.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.000
We'll look at the board of directors of a
company.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:34.000
We're going to be looking at insider stock
and stock option ownership and also the
trading of insider stock and stock
options.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:44.000
And we're also going to be looking at
research analyst estimates and the analyst
range and ratings for stocks.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.000
So let's start with earnings call
transcripts.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:55.000
It's a very important resource when going
through the qualitative process for any
stock.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:05.000
So once you've gone through financial
statements and company presentations and
you've identified company KPIs,

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:11.000
what you need to do now is tie up all of
the loose ends around those KPIs.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:26.000
So you can actually typically get a lot or
all of the main KPIs from earnings call
transcripts if they're not available on
the investor relations website.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:40.000
This is always on a case by case basis and
in a particular situation, a particular
stock management may not be communicating
those KPIs to the market in written form,

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.000
but they may be communicating them
verbally.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:49.000
This is you tying up the loose ends by
looking at earnings call transcripts.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.000
So you have to double check them.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:03:00.000
So to make sure that there's no stone left
unturned, go straight to the earnings call
transcripts next.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.000
So what is an actual earnings call
transcript?

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:10.000
Well, it's when management communicate
their results on a conference call

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:20.000
and they do this verbally to shareholders
and the public on the day that the company
releases their quarterly earnings report.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:27.000
And these earnings calls are transcribed
and they are uploaded to the company
website.

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.000
They're uploaded online and they are made
publicly available.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:39.000
So usually what you're going to see is
you're going to see the head of investor
relations hosting the conference call.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:46.000
And both the CEO and the CFO will
literally tell you what numbers to focus
on.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:52.000
Now, of course, we have to use a bit of
common sense here and be a little bit
cautious.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:57.000
Of course, earnings calls are scripted and
rehearsed.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:02.000
It's the information that management wants
you to focus on.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:08.000
However, at this stage of our analysis for
our purposes right now,

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:19.000
it's simply to identify the KPIs that we
haven't identified yet from company
presentations.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:27.000
The trick here and to do it quickly and
efficiently is to scan the earnings call
transcript

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:39.000
and just let your eye zoom in on the
numbers and pay less attention to all of
the mumbo jumbo and noise in the rest of
the transcript.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:42.000
So how do you actually go about doing
this?

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.000
Well, we've got an example here.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.000
We're looking at Abercrombie and Fitch,
ANF.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:51.000
We're looking at their KPIs and management
operating plan.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:59.000
And this is where we're going to be doing
further work using this example of ANF,
Abercrombie and Fitch.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.000
What you're going to do is use some common
sense.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:09.000
You're going to get a great shortcut by
just going to Google and typing in the
stock or the stock code

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.000
with the extension earnings call
transcripts.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:23.000
And you'll get a lot of search results and
most probably the most recent ones right
at the top of the page on Google.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:28.000
There's obviously going to be multiple
sources that document this information.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:32.000
What we want you to focus on here is
obviously the process.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.000
In years from now, the process will still
be the same.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.000
Sources may be different.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:49.000
But right now we have a search result at
the top of the page where we can see on
the top of the search results on Google
website seekingalpha.com.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:54.000
And we just click through and we take a
look.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.000
And what do we find when we click through?

00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:07.000
Yes, we get a full history of Abercrombie
and Fitch quarterly earnings and earnings
call transcripts.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:21.000
Not only that, but we also get the full
presentation released by the company that
accompanies the transcribed upload of the
earnings call.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:36.000
So here we have clicked through on the top
result and we get to the page where we
have the Abercrombie and Fitch Q4 2020
earnings call transcript.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:41.000
And we can see who was on the call from
the company.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:43.000
So the company participants.

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:53.000
And not only that, we can see other
conference call participants, namely the
research analysts at the various
investment banks.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:07:00.000
And as well, both research companies and
brokerage companies.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:12.000
What you will find is that the format of
earnings calls, earnings conference calls
across the market, across various stocks,
follow pretty much the same format.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:19.000
So the head of investor relations or
representative of investor relations will
introduce the call.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:23.000
And then the CEO and CFO will come on
later.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:24.000
And what do we see here?

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.000
And what do we see here?

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:32.000
We see Pam Quintiliano, who's from
investor relations, introducing the call.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:39.000
And then after the introduction, turning
it over to the CEO, Fran Horowitz.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:42.000
Now, what are we interested in?

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:52.000
We are obviously interested in identifying
key performance indicators, KPIs from the
earnings call transcripts.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:58.000
We're looking for what's important from
the language and where they are pointing
us.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:01.000
What direction are they pointing us in?

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:09.000
What numbers are they pointing us at that
are important as business drivers behind
revenue and earnings?

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:24.000
Well, what you will find in most cases,
the vast, vast majority of cases, is that
the CEO will start pointing you in the
direction of the KPIs that matter.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:32.000
So if we look at what Fran Horowitz starts
talking about immediately, what's Fran
actually talking about?

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:36.000
Well, here's what she's saying.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:49.000
So if we just look at the paragraph, her
opening paragraph where she starts talking
about numbers after the niceties are out
of the way, she says,

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:56.000
Looking back on 2020, I am proud of our
accomplishments and the positive change
that we drove.

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:57.000
Okay?

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:58.000
That's great.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.000
That's all mumbo jumbo so far.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.000
There's no numbers around that.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:09.000
Then she gets into the number, the numbers
that she wants to talk about immediately
that are important.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.000
So here's what she's saying.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:14.000
Despite the difficult operating
environment.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:17.000
Yes, 2020 was the COVID year.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:27.000
She's saying we grew digital penetration
to 54% of revenue from 33% last year.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:31.000
So using common sense, what does that tell
us?

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:37.000
Digital penetration is obviously an
important KPI to this company.

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:39.000
How do we know that?

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:46.000
Because literally the CEO is pointing to
it immediately in the earnings conference
call.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:49.000
It's the first number that she mentions.

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:54.000
So it has to be an important number.

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.000
Additionally, in that paragraph, what
she's saying.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:08.000
She's saying they removed 1.1 million
gross square feet or 17% from our global
base.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:09.000
What does that mean?

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:12.000
It means they are closing retail stores.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:19.000
They are reducing their retail store
capacity by 17%.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:24.000
So taking that into context with digital
penetration increasing.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:25.000
What does that mean?

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:31.000
They are moving from physical stores to
online sales.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:42.000
These are two obviously very important
KPIs to the current management operating
plan of Abercrombie and Fitch.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:50.000
Additionally, she is saying that they
expanded their gross profit margin by 110
basis points.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:11:05.000
Which we know is the end result of KPIs
such as increasing digital penetration and
decreasing gross square feet for retail
stores.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:11.000
Gross profit margin is an end result based
on revenue and earnings.

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:24.000
So what she's saying here is that the
switch from physical retail stores to
digital and online sales has resulted in
an increase in gross profit margin.

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:35.000
Additionally, she is saying that they
fortified their balance sheet to the end
of the year and they've got 1.3 billion
dollars of liquidity.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:48.000
Now what that definition of liquidity
means, you would go and have to do further
work on, but you have all of the balance
sheet metrics from earlier in the video
series to enable you to work that out.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:52.000
But again, this is a end result metric.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:55.000
This is something that ends up in the
financial statements.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:57.000
It's not a KPI.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:08.000
Remember KPIs are measures of performance
of the business that is driving revenue
and earnings.

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:20.000
So what we take from this so far on the
first page of the earnings call transcript
is that the increase and the rate of
increase of digital penetration to online
sales.

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:32.000
Versus removing or reducing gross square
feet for retail stores are important KPIs.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:37.000
If we continue through the earnings call
transcript, we can go through some more
examples.

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:52.000
If we just allow our eye to zoom in to
what really matters, we can see Fran is
going through information here pertaining
to digital.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:13:04.000
So results she's saying were driven by
digital, which grew 34% to $639 million,
making up 57% of sales.

00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:09.000
They've continued to get year on year
improvements in website traffic.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:16.000
And all of this has resulted in the best
digital gross margin rate in eight years.

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:31.000
We also start finding in the earnings call
transcript references to some acronyms
that we would actually obviously go and
have to look into further.

00:13:31.000 --> 00:13:37.000
So she's saying here that we realized
higher year over year AUR.

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:43.000
Obviously you would go away and figure
that out and figure out what it is.

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:56.000
No matter what sector you're in, no matter
what stocks you're looking at, you're
obviously going to have to do a bit of
extra work, a bit of digging to find out
the definitions of certain metrics.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:08.000
We also have a reference to inventories
and the level of inventories down 7% on a
5% revenue decline.

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:15.000
Inventories are a classic KPI in the
retail sector and obviously quite
important.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:21.000
And also the quality of inventories is
also important as well.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:32.000
What we also pick up on here is a
reference to all four brands that the
company has in its portfolio.

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:36.000
So yes, this may be new information at
this stage.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:50.000
So we pick up that the amount of brands in
a portfolio and how profitable those
brands are in aggregate and relative to
each other is a KPI that's important as
well.

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:59.000
We also pick up on another KPI called
average transaction value.

00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:04.000
And that is from both new and existing
customers.

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:11.000
We make a note of that and we put it in
our KPI list and we do more work on it.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:29.000
We can see as well that there's also a
reference to a metric and it's actually an
estimate for a metric called new to file
customers in digital penetration, digital
growth.

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:34.000
And she's referencing that the channel
grew over 35%.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:48.000
Again, this metric or this estimate of new
to file customers, this definition of new
to file customers, you would go away,
figure out what the definition of that
actually is.

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:57.000
And if it's a genuine KPI, stick it in
your KPI list and do more work on it and
see how it's been trending over time.

00:15:57.000 --> 00:16:06.000
Then on the next page, we see a reference
to a focus on assortment architecture.

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:21.000
That's really just a fancy way of saying
in retail land what items they are
marketing, what items they are pushing and
the mix of the sales from the items that
they are selling.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:33.000
And she's saying that the strategy, the
focus has been on selling high margin must
haves and their top 30 items.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:38.000
And it's continuing to pay off in terms of
strategy.

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:50.000
So with this particular operating plan,
this would actually be part of an overall
management operating plan, but it can't
really be a key performance.

00:16:50.000 --> 00:17:01.000
Indicator because we can't put numbers
around that moving forward in the earnings
call transcript to the next page.

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:13.000
We see a reference again to the decrease
in square footage of retail or physical
stores, but now we're getting a bit more
meat on the bone.

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:30.000
So she's telling us that in 2020, there
was a closure of 137 locations, which
added up to 1.1 million gross square feet
or the 17% of the base that we saw in the,
on the first page.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:48.000
And she's also telling us that they closed
129 of the 137 locations, 129 non flagship
stores, which made up 850,000 of the 1.1
million.

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:50.000
So what can we infer from that?

00:17:50.000 --> 00:18:02.000
Well, we can infer potentially that the
strategy here in detail is to close non
flagship stores and keep the flagship
stores open.

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:18.000
So again, we've got plenty of KPIs and
numbers to work with here.

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:23.000
We've got our gross square feet number,
the percentage of the base.

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:30.000
So that's the speed per quarter at which
they're reducing and what they're actually
reducing.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:33.000
We've got the amount of locations.

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:45.000
We can use that as a metric, a KPI going
forward and the amount of non flagship
stores and flagship stores from those
locations.

00:18:45.000 --> 00:19:01.000
We can use them as a KPI as well and use
them going forward to measure the
effectiveness of the management operating
plan and to gauge an expectation of
revenue and earnings in the future.

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:14.000
Again, the CEO here is literally telling
us what's important to the management
operating plan by saying aligning square
footage with digital penetration

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:21.000
is the most important lever to maintain
and improve profitability.

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:38.000
So guys, she is literally telling you here
what the management operating plan is, how
they're actually going to achieve an
increase in revenue and earnings and all
of the numbers to focus on in achieving
that.

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:59.000
Now, the great thing about earnings call
transcripts and going through this process
is that many of the KPIs that you're going
to obtain by doing this work on a
particular stock are going to be the same
KPIs for many other stocks in the sector.

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:00.000
There is definitely going to be a large
overlap.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:04.000
There is definitely going to be a large
overlap.

00:20:04.000 --> 00:20:24.000
So the good news, the good thing about
this is that you only have to do the work
once on a stock, create your KPI dashboard
and many of the KPIs that you have on your
dashboard are going to apply across the
sector to other stocks.

00:20:24.000 --> 00:20:43.000
Now, of course, what you're going to find
is when you do this work and go through
the processes, additional to KPIs that
apply across the sector, there are also
going to be KPIs that are idiosyncratic to
that particular stock.

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:58.000
You have to be mindful of this and
separate out KPIs that apply across the
sector to other stocks and KPIs that apply
only to a particular stock.

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:09.000
So when you're identifying KPIs for a
particular stock, you can obviously also
compare them to other stocks in the same
sector.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:19.000
And of course, always be mindful why
you're at this stage in the first place,
why you're actually at the qualitative
stage and doing the work.

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:28.000
It's because you've identified a positive
or negative outlier from the quantitative
phase.

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:32.000
So always be asking yourself that question
in the back of your mind.

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:33.000
It's good discipline.

00:21:33.000 --> 00:21:40.000
Why is this stock on a premium or a
discount to the sector?

00:21:40.000 --> 00:21:44.000
It may lie in a particular KPI.

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:53.000
The answer may lie in one of the KPIs or a
few of the KPIs that you're looking at,
whether they are good or bad.

00:21:53.000 --> 00:21:57.000
Now, I know what you'll be thinking at
this stage.

00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:12.000
Wouldn't it be great if I could get my
hands on a book or a cheat sheet or a
shortcut or a hack to know all of the KPIs
in every single sector forever throughout
time.

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:32.000
And you may be thinking of asking us the
question, Anton, will you be providing me
with all the KPIs for every GIX and NAICS
sector so I never have to do any work or
learn the information myself or go through
any processes myself?

00:22:32.000 --> 00:22:39.000
Well, the answer to that question,
unfortunately, is a flat no.

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:40.000
No.

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:41.000
Why?

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:48.000
Because over time, KPIs may change and you
need to learn the process.

00:22:48.000 --> 00:22:51.000
That's the most important thing.

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:55.000
At the end of the day, you have to be self
sustainable.

00:22:55.000 --> 00:23:08.000
You have to have a good, solid work ethic
and you have to know and understand how to
process yourself so you can work smart and
not hard.

00:23:08.000 --> 00:23:22.000
Don't be one of these people who always
looks for excuses and refuses to do the
work because you think you're entitled to
a shortcut and for everybody else to do
the work for you.

00:23:22.000 --> 00:23:37.000
Unfortunately, there's no way you can
climb the competency hierarchy and become
professional trader level unless you do
all of these processes yourself and they
become second nature to you.

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:56.000
Now, seeing as though we are in the retail
sector, let's have a look at a dashboard
that we may have gotten if we went through
the Abercrombie and Fitch processes for
qualitative analysis.

00:23:56.000 --> 00:24:05.000
So if you went through the processes
where, for example, you went through the
Abercrombie and Fitch financial
statements.

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:06.000
So quarterlies and annuals.

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:09.000
So quarterlies and annuals.

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:14.000
You went through company presentations
from the investor relations website.

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:24.000
And you also went through a number of
earnings call transcripts, conference call
transcripts in the past.

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:29.000
You may have got to a dashboard that looks
something like this.

00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:40.000
So here we've split it up and organized it
into categories of sales, inventory,
stores, brands and customers.

00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:45.000
So in sales, we have same store sales,
sales per square foot.

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:49.000
We have that AUR metric that we identified
earlier.

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:52.000
That is actually average unit retail.

00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:54.000
You can look at that definition in your
own time.

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:57.000
We have average sales per customer.

00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:01.000
And we have online sales percentage versus
physical location.

00:25:01.000 --> 00:25:05.000
So that's all in the category of sales.

00:25:05.000 --> 00:25:16.000
In the category of inventory, we have an
average inventory number, inventory
turnover, stock turnover days and sell
through rates.

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:23.000
For physical retail stores, we have number
of locations at the end and beginning of
the final hour.

00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:26.000
We have the number of locations at the
beginning of the financial period in
question.

00:25:26.000 --> 00:25:31.000
We have the number of company owned
locations, number of franchises.

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:36.000
We have total space owned by the company
in square feet.

00:25:36.000 --> 00:25:41.000
And we have total space leased by the
company in square feet.

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:46.000
And we have gross square feet for
aggregate retail stores.

00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:54.000
Within the category of brands, we have the
number of brands within a portfolio.

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:57.000
And we have the number of store names.

00:25:57.000 --> 00:26:02.000
And within customers, we have that metric
that we identified earlier.

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:04.000
New to file customers.

00:26:04.000 --> 00:26:08.000
That is customers that are new in the
database.

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:21.000
So whenever you are doing your work in the
qualitative analysis phase, going through
these processes for a particular stock,
what's going to happen?

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:26.000
You're going to end up with a dashboard
that looks something like this.

00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:32.000
And this applies to any stock in any
sector.

00:26:32.000 --> 00:26:44.000
For a particular stock in a particular
sector, you're going to categorize all of
the KPIs across the different categories
that they relate to.

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:54.000
What we are interested in as traders in
the qualitative phase is metrics that we
can actually put numbers to.

00:26:54.000 --> 00:27:04.000
So we want to be able to measure with
numbers, with data, through time, KPIs and
performance of the business.

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:22.000
And use those KPIs to infer a management
operating plan and to predict with a
probabilistic analysis the trend and an
expectation of positive or negative
surprise in the future.

00:27:22.000 --> 00:27:30.000
And what we mean by that is a positive or
negative surprise to future revenue and
earnings.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:41.000
That is the end result of KPIs trending
positively or negatively or even turning.

00:27:41.000 --> 00:27:59.000
So Abercrombie & Fitch, Q4 numbers, Q4
earnings called transcript and
presentations were a classic example for
traders and portfolio managers to be on
top of KPIs and an inferred management
operating plan.

00:27:59.000 --> 00:28:08.000
If you go back a number of slides in the
video, you will see that Abercrombie &
Fitch reported their full year.

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:24.000
And Q4 numbers, fourth quarter numbers for
2020, they reported them on the date the
2nd of March 2021.

00:28:24.000 --> 00:28:39.000
And if we just look at a stock chart from
the time, we can have a look at the
performance of Abercrombie & Fitch ANF
stock during that time period.

00:28:39.000 --> 00:29:01.000
So if we just segment this performance
based on quarters, if we have a look at Q4
2020, we're looking at the months October,
November, December, we can pretty much say
that the stock went from $15 a share to
$20 a share in that quarter, even above
$20.

00:29:01.000 --> 00:29:16.000
But being conservative, if we were on top
of the KPIs, but being conservative, if we
were on top of the KPIs, on top of the
trend, on top of the quantitative, and
also on top of our macro trends, we
probably would have captured that move.

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:23.000
And that is a $5 per share move on a $15
starting point.

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:25.000
So $15 to $20.

00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:29.000
So we're getting the stock price moving up
by 33%.

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:54.000
If we then look at the first quarter in
2021 after getting a sideways movement in
December in 2020, we can see that the
stock rallies very significantly all the
way into full year and Q4 numbers, which
remember were the 2nd of March 2021.

00:29:54.000 --> 00:30:03.000
And you can see after they report, the
stock actually rallies quite a lot as
well.

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:23.000
So again, like in Q4 2020, if in Q1 2021,
we were on top of everything here, our
macro, our quantitative, our qualitative,
our KPIs, and we were on top of the trend
of what was happening in this stock.

00:30:23.000 --> 00:30:26.000
We knew the management operating plan.

00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:27.000
We were on top of it.

00:30:27.000 --> 00:30:36.000
We may have had a position going into
numbers because revenue and earnings is
the end result.

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:55.000
So if we got into this stock in January
2021 at say 22 and a half, and then we got
out of it at say 32 and a half in March,
we would have made around 45%.

00:30:55.000 --> 00:31:12.000
And yes, in both of these quarters,
Abercrombie and Fitch, as an example, in
both of these quarters, this fits within
our 20 to 60 day time horizon, obviously,
because we are segmenting by quarters.

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:22.000
And for the record, Abercrombie and Fitch
would have been categorized in our
analysis as a turnaround story.

00:31:22.000 --> 00:31:33.000
This stock back in 2007 and in 2011 was
trading at above $70 a share.

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:44.000
And from 2011 onwards, the company, the
stock had a miserable decade and the
turnaround didn't actually come until
2021.

00:31:44.000 --> 00:32:06.000
So Abercrombie and Fitch, classic example
of a turnaround story and a great example
of identifying KPIs, inferring a
management operating plan, and also how
everything fits together from our
qualitative analysis, our quantitative
analysis as well.

00:32:06.000 --> 00:32:16.000
And also with an assumption, a macro
assumption in the background that we were
leaning towards being bullish.

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:35.000
So that is earnings called transcripts
guys, fit them in, obviously with your
previous analysis for the qualitative
phase, when you're looking at financial
statements, and also company
presentations, company presentations and
transcripts.

00:32:35.000 --> 00:32:45.000
Definitely the most important in
identifying KPIs and inferring a
management operating plan.

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:51.000
When you're identifying KPIs and inferring
the management operating plan, we want
metrics.

00:32:51.000 --> 00:32:52.000
We want data.

00:32:52.000 --> 00:32:54.000
We want meat on the bone.

00:32:54.000 --> 00:33:03.000
We want to be able to quantify with
numbers, what is driving revenue and
earnings.

00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:10.000
What is behind revenue and earnings and
creating revenue and earnings.

00:33:10.000 --> 00:33:14.000
How is the company actually measuring its
business.

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:25.000
And when you get good at this, you'll be
able to do it very, very quickly and
create your KPI and inferred MOP
management operating plan dashboard.

00:33:25.000 --> 00:33:37.000
Now, something else that we need to look
at that is part of the qualitative
assessment is management track record.

00:33:37.000 --> 00:33:50.000
Now, when we're looking at the management
of a public company, this is obviously not
like identifying KPIs where we can put
numbers to the business and our analysis.

00:33:50.000 --> 00:34:07.000
It does fall into the category of
qualitative work, and it gives us a very
good idea of the quality of the team
behind the numbers, behind the business.

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:14.000
And here, we're going to have a look at an
example where we're looking at the company
HubSpot.

00:34:14.000 --> 00:34:22.000
And with any company, you'll be able to
get the leadership team, any public
company, you'll be able to get it on their
website.

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:33.000
And you'll be able to look into the
backgrounds of all the key leadership
team, all the key management team at the
top of the tree.

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:48.000
And additional to the company website,
there's multiple resources online,
obviously, where you can dig around about
the backgrounds of all the key leadership
members of the team.

00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:56.000
These guys are at the top of the tree of a
public company, and therefore, they are
publicly facing.

00:34:56.000 --> 00:35:01.000
So, you're going to get information on
these guys publicly.

00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:03.000
It's going to be online.

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:11.000
You can Google around, and you can access
many resources to get as much information
as you can as possible.

00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:22.000
Additionally, there's a certain lens in
which you would view a management team or
leadership team through as a trader
portfolio manager.

00:35:22.000 --> 00:35:29.000
And there's certain processes that you
would go through to learn all of this
qualitative information properly.

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:35.000
And we're going to go through these steps
now with HubSpot as the example.

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:45.000
So, the first thing you would do is
identify who is the alpha, who is the CEO,
who's running the company.

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:49.000
And you would ask a whole bunch of
questions of that person.

00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:52.000
How long have they been with the company?

00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:56.000
What is his or her relevant experience?

00:35:56.000 --> 00:35:59.000
Have they actually led a team before?

00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:01.000
And if so, which teams?

00:36:01.000 --> 00:36:03.000
What were the success rates?

00:36:03.000 --> 00:36:08.000
Have they been involved in previous
corporate success stories?

00:36:08.000 --> 00:36:13.000
How much stock and stock options do they
actually own?

00:36:13.000 --> 00:36:18.000
Are they invested in the success, the
future success of the company?

00:36:18.000 --> 00:36:21.000
And also, what pedigree do they have?

00:36:21.000 --> 00:36:23.000
Where did they go to school?

00:36:23.000 --> 00:36:26.000
And what did they actually study at
school?

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:35.000
These are the questions that you should
ask of all key management personnel that
are in leadership roles in a public
company.

00:36:36.000 --> 00:36:41.000
So, here for HubSpot, we have the CEO,
Brian Halligan.

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:45.000
And he's not just the CEO of the company.

00:36:45.000 --> 00:36:51.000
He is also the co-founder, which tells us
there were other co-founders as well.

00:36:51.000 --> 00:36:57.000
So, Brian Halligan, he's a co-founder and
the current CEO of HubSpot.

00:36:58.000 --> 00:37:00.000
So, how long has he been at the company?

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:02.000
He's been there since the very beginning.

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:06.000
Now, what's his actual experience?

00:37:06.000 --> 00:37:20.000
Well, previously, before starting HubSpot,
he was a venture partner at Longworth
Ventures and a VP of Sales at Groove
Networks, which was acquired by Microsoft.

00:37:21.000 --> 00:37:26.000
So, if we're asking these questions,
what's his relevant experience?

00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:28.000
Has he actually led a team before?

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:34.000
And has he been involved in previous
corporate success stories?

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:37.000
The answers are very obvious.

00:37:38.000 --> 00:37:41.000
He's got experience in the industry.

00:37:41.000 --> 00:37:43.000
He's led teams.

00:37:43.000 --> 00:37:50.000
And he's also had previous corporate
success by building a company and selling
it to Microsoft.

00:37:50.000 --> 00:37:57.000
Now, we're going to get on to stock and
stock options ownership a bit later on.

00:37:57.000 --> 00:38:01.000
But answering the last question, where do
they go to school?

00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:03.000
And what did they actually do at school?

00:38:04.000 --> 00:38:08.000
Well, he went to the University of Vermont
and did a BSEE.

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:13.000
And he did an MBA at MIT's Sloan School of
Management.

00:38:13.000 --> 00:38:23.000
Where now he's also a senior lecturer and
he's teaching a course called Scaling
Entrepreneurial Ventures.

00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:33.000
So, as we're going through our qualitative
work, our first step, things are looking
pretty good with Brian Halligan as the CEO
of HubSpot.

00:38:34.000 --> 00:38:45.000
So, all of our questions that we've seeked
answers to so far that we can get answers
to at this stage have been answered
positively.

00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:54.000
So, the next step we would take, after
we've looked at the CEO, is to look at
other people in management.

00:38:54.000 --> 00:39:00.000
And the next step we take is to identify
primary or key management positions.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:04.000
And what we're trying to do is identify
the primary beaters.

00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:07.000
These are our lieutenants.

00:39:07.000 --> 00:39:10.000
The primary beaters are our lieutenants.

00:39:10.000 --> 00:39:21.000
They are the key members of the management
team that are actually, on a daily basis,
implementing the plans of management.

00:39:21.000 --> 00:39:33.000
So, in the HubSpot example, we start with
this guy, Dharmesh Shah, who's Chief
Technical Officer, and he's also a
co-founder.

00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:46.000
So, there's a question mark actually here
over Dharmesh Shah, whether he is a legacy
alpha or not, whether he is subservient to
the CEO or not.

00:39:46.000 --> 00:39:49.000
We would have to do further work on that.

00:39:50.000 --> 00:40:02.000
But certainly, the rest of these guys
outside Dharmesh Shah, we can pretty much
say with high probability that these guys
are management lieutenants, senior
management lieutenants.

00:40:02.000 --> 00:40:04.000
They are primary beaters.

00:40:05.000 --> 00:40:09.000
And they report to the alpha, Brian
Halligan.

00:40:10.000 --> 00:40:14.000
So, all of these guys and their
backgrounds are very, very important.

00:40:14.000 --> 00:40:19.000
It's very important that the primary
beaters are extremely effective.

00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:23.000
So, we start with the CFO, the Chief
Financial Officer.

00:40:24.000 --> 00:40:27.000
And in the HubSpot example, this is Kate
Bucher.

00:40:27.000 --> 00:40:32.000
So, we're answering the question here, who
is actually looking after the books?

00:40:33.000 --> 00:40:39.000
Next up, we've got the Chief Product
Officer, which is Christopher O'Donnell.

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:45.000
He obviously has a very important role in
the company at management level.

00:40:46.000 --> 00:40:51.000
And he is spearheading product development
at HubSpot.

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:53.000
Why is he really important?

00:40:54.000 --> 00:40:59.000
Well, in any business without a product,
there simply are no customers.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:05.000
Next up is the Chief Architect, a guy
called Whitney Sorensen.

00:41:05.000 --> 00:41:15.000
In this business, he's extremely important
at the management level because he is
spearheading the engineering effort for
the product.

00:41:16.000 --> 00:41:23.000
So, the engineering effort behind the
product and without good engineering,
there is no product.

00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:29.000
So, HubSpot is a CRM, a client
relationship management tool.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:38.000
And if you want a first class product in
that industry, you need first class
engineering.

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:45.000
So, the Chief Architect, Whitney Sorensen,
is definitely a key member of the
management team

00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:50.000
and is a Lieutenant Primary Beta reporting
to the CEO.

00:41:51.000 --> 00:41:59.000
Next up at Primary Beta management level
is Corporate and Business Development,
Andrew Lindsay.

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:08.000
This guy is in charge of the expansion
strategy of the company, which is
obviously extremely key to growing the
business.

00:42:09.000 --> 00:42:16.000
And next up after that is the CMO, Chief
Marketing Officer, Kip Bodnar.

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:26.000
In any business, in any industry, for a
particular company, growth is simply not
possible without killer marketing.

00:42:27.000 --> 00:42:35.000
So, we go through the phase of identifying
our key lieutenants, our primary betas
that report to the alpha.

00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:45.000
And then we also, by default, identify
secondary betas, so secondary or less than
key management positions.

00:42:46.000 --> 00:42:53.000
These guys also report to the CEO, but
their roles are less important than the
primary betas.

00:42:54.000 --> 00:43:04.000
So, we have our Chief Customer Officer,
Chief People Officer, Executive VP Revenue
Operations, General Counsel,

00:43:05.000 --> 00:43:11.000
Executive VP Customer Success, and Chief
Security Officer, and Senior VP for
Engineering.

00:43:12.000 --> 00:43:17.000
So, the lens in the way that you have to
view management and the processes that you
go through,

00:43:18.000 --> 00:43:27.000
at the management level, you need to
identify who the key people are below the
CEO.

00:43:27.000 --> 00:43:29.000
So, who are the primary betas?

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:39.000
And also, identify who are the hangers-on
or the people who have pretty much token
positions at management level.

00:43:40.000 --> 00:43:42.000
So, who are the secondary betas?

00:43:43.000 --> 00:43:44.000
Why?

00:43:44.000 --> 00:43:53.000
Well, because it's the alpha and the
primary betas that drive the product, the
management operating plan, and the KPIs.

00:43:54.000 --> 00:44:00.000
And this is what ultimately drives revenue
and earnings, and the success of the
company.

00:44:01.000 --> 00:44:12.000
And for the record, in the hierarchy,
beneath the alpha, we identify the primary
and secondary betas,

00:44:12.000 --> 00:44:15.000
but then there's also tertiary betas.

00:44:15.000 --> 00:44:23.000
So, these are lower-level management
personnel, and we also have the workers,
which we call deltas.

00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:31.000
So, when you're going to look at a
company's management, and you're actually
appraising the management track record,

00:44:32.000 --> 00:44:36.000
you have to go through a process of
identifying who the key guys are.

00:44:37.000 --> 00:44:48.000
So, this is a good template to use,
identify the alpha, split up the betas,
the lieutenants into primary and secondary
betas,

00:44:49.000 --> 00:44:54.000
and appraise their backgrounds, appraise
their management track record.

00:44:55.000 --> 00:45:01.000
In the HubSpot example, we can look at all
of the primary betas.

00:45:02.000 --> 00:45:11.000
You can get all the information on their
backgrounds on the company website, and
you can even just start Googling their
names as well,

00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:16.000
and hoarding information to appraise their
management track record.

00:45:17.000 --> 00:45:27.000
Something else that you would likely be
very interested in when you're going
through the qualitative phase is who sits
on the board.

00:45:28.000 --> 00:45:30.000
Who are the board of directors?

00:45:31.000 --> 00:45:45.000
And something that you have to understand
here is that for a public company, the
board of directors is not supposed to be
subservient to the CEO and management.

00:45:46.000 --> 00:45:56.000
What you have to look for when you're
assessing the board of directors is you
need to look for evidence of political
incest amongst betas.

00:45:57.000 --> 00:46:06.000
How many of the betas are sitting on the
board, what is the overlap, and have a
look at additional personnel on the board
as well.

00:46:07.000 --> 00:46:16.000
And what relationships they may or may not
have had in the past with primary and
secondary betas in management.

00:46:17.000 --> 00:46:26.000
At the end of the day, the board of
directors is supposed to keep the CEO and
management in check.

00:46:27.000 --> 00:46:31.000
So high integrity is required from the
board of directors.

00:46:32.000 --> 00:46:36.000
We don't want to see lots of evidence of
political incest.

00:46:37.000 --> 00:46:42.000
We only want to really see that if we're
thinking about shorting a stock.

00:46:43.000 --> 00:46:51.000
So if we're looking at a negative outlier,
we would want to see evidence of political
incest.

00:46:52.000 --> 00:47:02.000
But certainly, if we're looking to go
long, if we've identified a positive
outlier in a sector, we need high
integrity from the board.

00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:07.000
So what do we get from HubSpot as our
example?

00:47:08.000 --> 00:47:12.000
Well, we get a great situation.

00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:26.000
We have great product, great engineering,
an awesome marketing effort, a very solid
expansion strategy, and across management,
we have a very strong management
background.

00:47:27.000 --> 00:47:34.000
And as a bonus, we also have a board of
directors with high integrity.

00:47:35.000 --> 00:47:35.000
And guess what?

00:47:36.000 --> 00:47:46.000
This has all manifested itself into an
awesome business, an awesome company, and
a very, very good performing stock.

00:47:47.000 --> 00:48:12.000
So obviously, when we look at stock price
performance, as we've looked at before in
multiple examples, in the context of a
trade idea, we obviously make some
assumptions here that at the time, you
will have been bullish on the macro
environment with a net long bias in your
portfolio.

00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:27.000
You will have been bullish on the sector,
and you will have identified a particular
stock, in this example, HubSpot, as a
positive outlier in the sector.

00:48:27.000 --> 00:48:34.000
So through your quant analysis,
identifying HubSpot as a positive outlier
in the sector.

00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:55.000
And then, of course, you will have moved
on to the next level, and done the
qualitative analysis, and then decided the
timing was good, and you either bought the
stock as an outright long, or structured
it as a long option structure, utilizing
call options.

00:48:56.000 --> 00:49:20.000
And if we look at two periods in recent
history from the time of recording, so if
we look at Q4 2020, and we look at Q1
2021, then we'll see some outperformance
and some big opportunity back then for
absolute return.

00:49:21.000 --> 00:49:47.000
So if you imagine yourself sitting in
October 2020, so the beginning of October
2020, and you've done all your work on
HubSpot, it's not unreasonable to assume
that in Q4, you would have bought this
stock at around 300 or in the low 300s,
and sold it either in the high 300s or
around $400.

00:49:47.000 --> 00:50:04.000
So within our 20 to 60 day time horizon,
as long short portfolio managers, and
returning roughly a 30, 33% return,
something like this.

00:50:04.000 --> 00:50:13.000
So if you can see if you were either
coming into the new year in 2021, and
you'd missed that move, that's okay.

00:50:13.000 --> 00:50:21.000
You may have come in and done your work
and realized that HubSpot was probably
still a buy at the time.

00:50:21.000 --> 00:50:42.000
You may have also been in the position in
Q4 and traded out, and then you were faced
with the choice of looking to take a
position in something else, or even
restructuring the trade given the new
fundamentals or situation at the time in
the market.

00:50:42.000 --> 00:50:57.000
It doesn't really matter if you just think
at the end of Q4, beginning of Q1, you
were faced with the choices, and one of
the choices is to just buy HubSpot or not.

00:50:57.000 --> 00:51:06.000
And buying it for the 20 to 60 day time
horizon, obviously not as an investment.

