01:26:10.460 --> 01:26:18.120
So let's talk here briefly about the
descriptors of ratings themselves.

01:26:18.520 --> 01:26:25.240
So there's essentially three ratings an
analyst can actually give a stock.

01:26:25.640 --> 01:26:33.560
It's either going to be a buy rating, a
hold rating, which can also be described
as neutral, or a sell rating.

01:26:33.600 --> 01:26:37.940
And strong buy, and strong buy, just means
buy, okay?

01:26:38.400 --> 01:26:41.500
And underperform, just means sell.

01:26:41.960 --> 01:26:46.400
So if you see the term outperform, it just
means buy.

01:26:46.400 --> 01:26:54.640
If you see the term underperform, or even
strong sell, it just means sell.

01:26:55.060 --> 01:27:04.420
So the way to look at this is always just
think of it in terms of ratings of buy,
hold, or neutral, or sell.

01:27:04.420 --> 01:27:16.820
And what you've also got to remember is
that these ratings are not referring to
outperforming or underperforming the
general stock market.

01:27:17.020 --> 01:27:23.500
It's with reference to a relative call on
the stock to the stock's sector.

01:27:24.700 --> 01:27:34.540
So if you see a buy rating on a stock, it
does not mean that the analyst is saying
they think this stock is going to
outperform the market.

01:27:34.820 --> 01:27:39.360
It means they think that the stock is
going to outperform the sector.

01:27:40.260 --> 01:27:42.580
And of course, the opposite is true.

01:27:42.580 --> 01:27:57.920
If you locate a stock that has a sell
recommendation, an analyst has a sell
recommendation on that stock, it means the
analyst thinks it's going to underperform
the other stocks in the sector.

01:27:57.920 --> 01:28:00.920
So it's underperforming the sector
average.

01:28:01.920 --> 01:28:16.080
In addition, what you have to remember is
that when an analyst covers multiple
stocks in a sector, they have to have an
equal number of buy and sell
recommendations.

01:28:16.480 --> 01:28:27.480
They can't just have a distribution of
ratings with stocks across a sector where
they're just bullish everything or they
have everything on a hold recommendation.

01:28:27.480 --> 01:28:52.820
And it stands to reason if you have a
bunch of stocks in a sector, let's say,
for example, 20, from all of the estimates
out there, the consensus estimates for
forward-looking EPS, for revenue, etc.,
there's obviously going to be a mean
average.

01:28:52.820 --> 01:29:05.480
And at any one moment in time, there's
going to be stocks that contribute
positively to that average, and there's
going to be stocks that contribute
negatively to that average.

01:29:06.160 --> 01:29:10.300
It can never be the case that it's any
different to that.

01:29:10.560 --> 01:29:12.340
It's always going to be the case.

01:29:13.340 --> 01:29:26.440
So when analysts have buy ratings or sell
ratings, what they're actually saying to
you is that their buy ratings are stocks
that they believe will outperform the
sector average,

01:29:26.800 --> 01:29:32.900
and their sell ratings are stocks that
they believe will underperform the sector
average.

01:29:33.900 --> 01:29:45.260
Now, on top of that, they also have price
targets, and this also sets a range across
the analysts for price targets themselves.

01:29:46.060 --> 01:29:53.860
And in our example here with Abercrombie &
