We don't use one part of it.

01:49:21.200 --> 01:49:22.200
We don't use one part of it.

01:49:22.200 --> 01:49:30.000
From analysts and understanding how that
has evolved over time in the near past,
you are

01:49:30.000 --> 01:49:40.000
getting a handle on the current situation
of market expectation and on top of that,
you

01:49:40.000 --> 01:49:49.160
are getting a handle on whether there is
room for upgrades, downgrades or indeed
ratings changes

01:49:49.160 --> 01:49:53.920
in the future that may provide future
catalysts.

01:49:53.920 --> 01:50:04.720
You are understanding and identifying more
important qualitative parameters for the
stock itself.

01:50:04.720 --> 01:50:11.360
So in the future, if you have a position
in the stock, you can digest news that's
coming

01:50:11.360 --> 01:50:14.940
out far more effectively.

01:50:14.940 --> 01:50:22.940
So for example, with Abercrombie & Fitch,
if you went through all of this analysis,
if

01:50:22.940 --> 01:50:33.920
you recall, we had $1.50 of earnings per
share as an estimate from the market for
2022.

01:50:33.920 --> 01:50:43.560
We know that the current distribution of
analyst ratings is skewed towards a hold
rating, but

01:50:43.560 --> 01:50:48.920
it's somewhere between a hold and a buy
rating.

01:50:48.920 --> 01:50:58.020
And we know that the current stock price
is $37.49 and the average price target for
analysts

01:50:58.020 --> 01:51:02.780
on a very wide range is $34.11.

01:51:02.780 --> 01:51:12.460
So what happens if we have a position in
this stock and the company guides to an
EPS number

01:51:12.460 --> 01:51:19.660
for 2022 when they report their quarterly
earnings in 2021?

01:51:19.660 --> 01:51:24.420
What if they guide their 2022 number to
$1.75?

01:51:24.420 --> 01:51:33.060
Well, we are on top of everything and we
know that analysts are going to have to
upgrade

01:51:33.060 --> 01:51:36.260
the stock in response.

01:51:36.260 --> 01:51:42.180
They're all going to also have to up their
estimates and up their price targets.

01:51:42.180 --> 01:51:45.420
And the stock is going to go up a lot.

01:51:45.420 --> 01:51:55.600
So in terms of qualitative processing,
you've just got to be on top of research
analyst estimates

01:51:55.600 --> 01:51:59.260
and the range of those estimates.

01:51:59.260 --> 01:52:09.900
You need to be on top of the positive or
negative mean consensus skew relative to
midpoints.

01:52:09.900 --> 01:52:16.660
You need to be on top of recent history or
near term estimates changes.

01:52:16.660 --> 01:52:22.840
And of course, we like to see some
evidence that we've got the wind blowing
in the right

01:52:22.840 --> 01:52:25.080
direction.

01:52:25.080 --> 01:52:31.940
And you need to be on top of analyst
ratings, the distribution of those
ratings, how they've

01:52:31.940 --> 01:52:42.080
been changing through near past time, the
average rating in terms of the average of
the distribution

01:52:42.080 --> 01:52:45.440
and also price targets.

01:52:45.440 --> 01:52:52.620
All of this is going to keep you on top of
the parameters that you need to understand
for

01:52:52.620 --> 01:52:56.500
the stock in the real world.

01:52:56.500 --> 01:53:02.620
And moving forward through time, if you
have a position on, as the dynamic is
changing,

01:53:02.620 --> 01:53:08.880
you're going to understand how to digest
all of this information as fundamental
news comes

01:53:08.880 --> 01:53:13.820
out and these parameters move.

01:53:13.820 --> 01:53:22.360
Now we've obviously been through a lot in
this video and the previous video on
qualitative analysis.

01:53:22.360 --> 01:53:25.640
So let's summarize everything here.

01:53:25.640 --> 01:53:34.120
We obviously started with the most
important thing, which is identification
of KPIs.

01:53:34.120 --> 01:53:45.360
So again, we're not here going to give you
the entire list of KPIs for every sector
and subsector

01:53:45.360 --> 01:53:49.240
in the GICS or NAICS classifications.

01:53:49.240 --> 01:53:54.820
This is just something that you're going
to have to do for yourself by piecing the
evidence

01:53:54.820 --> 01:54:03.380
together from annual and quarterly
reports, company presentations, and
earnings call transcripts.

01:54:03.380 --> 01:54:10.220
And you need to do it on a case by case
basis until you get good at it.

01:54:10.220 --> 01:54:17.920
And when you start out, you're going to
end up with a full KPI list for a
particular stock.

01:54:17.920 --> 01:54:26.280
And by default, those same KPIs are going
to apply across the sector.

01:54:26.280 --> 01:54:33.560
And as you've seen, once you've identified
KPIs for a particular stock, by default,
you end

01:54:33.560 --> 01:54:39.240
up with an inferred management operating
plan for the stock.

01:54:39.240 --> 01:54:46.520
Now, not only KPIs for a particular stock,
an inferred management operating plan, and
also

01:54:46.520 --> 01:54:52.400
KPIs across a sector make up a full
qualitative analysis.

01:54:52.400 --> 01:55:11.200
You need to add in qualitative market
dynamics, such as management to themselves
and their track record, the board, insider
stock info, and an appreciation of
research analyst estimates, ranges, and
ratings.

01:55:12.580 --> 01:55:21.400
So, adding on all of this qualitative
analysis to your quantitative analysis is
absolutely essential, guys.

01:55:22.040 --> 01:55:25.980
And obviously, you need to be doing this
work yourself.

01:55:26.500 --> 01:55:28.520
You need to be going through all of this
yourself.

01:55:29.200 --> 01:55:38.980
Remember the ITPM principles,
self-sustainability, work ethic, free
information, and working smart, not hard.

01:55:40.580 --> 01:55:50.180
So, use videos 30 and 31 to achieve a full
qualitative analysis when you're doing
your work.

01:55:50.940 --> 01:56:05.840
Use them as a template and use the case
studies and apply the steps and processes
on a case-by-case basis until you build up
your own database of KPIs.

01:56:07.040 --> 01:56:10.440
And there's a discipline here to always
remember.

01:56:10.440 --> 01:56:28.900
When you're achieving a full qualitative
assessment of KPIs and an inferred
management operating plan, and also the
qualitative market dynamics, always refer
back to valuation from your quantitative
process.

01:56:28.900 --> 01:56:49.840
And ask the question, does the qualitative
assessment support or deny valuation and
my positive or negative outlier status
that I identified in the quantitative
process when I selected the stock to do
work on?

01:56:50.560 --> 01:57:00.040
When the qualitative assessment supports
our positive or negative outlier status,
we are in business.

01:57:00.040 --> 01:57:03.000
Our trade idea is in business.

01:57:03.240 --> 01:57:04.960
We then do further work.

01:57:05.960 --> 01:57:23.920
If our qualitative assessment denies our
valuation status, our positive or negative
outlier status, then we have a problem
because we are then clueless as to why
valuation is where it is.

01:57:23.920 --> 01:57:50.640
We can't take positions in stocks, long or
short, simply because we've identified
them as positive or negative outliers in
the quantitative phase without
understanding what is actually driving
valuation and having a full handle on it
and have an expectation for the future.

01:57:51.320 --> 01:57:57.840
We need our qualitative assessment to
actually support our valuation.

01:57:57.840 --> 01:58:05.640
And then we are moving one step closer to
having a full trade idea.

01:58:06.760 --> 01:58:14.420
So guys, those are the steps for the
qualitative analysis that we've covered in
the last two videos.

01:58:15.000 --> 01:58:17.460
Let's go back to the desk for a recap.

01:58:21.320 --> 01:58:25.360
Okay, guys, so welcome back to the desk
once again.

01:58:26.040 --> 01:58:28.200
The last few videos, what did we look at?

01:58:28.280 --> 01:58:30.500
We looked at qualitative analysis.

01:58:31.100 --> 01:58:33.980
So let's have a recap of what we looked at
the last few videos.

01:58:34.740 --> 01:58:41.260
So the first thing we looked at was KPIs,
key performance indicators, and actually
where to find them.

01:58:41.840 --> 01:58:46.980
Primarily, we're going to get them at the
investor relations website.

01:58:47.460 --> 01:58:50.520
However, let's use a little bit of common
sense here.

01:58:50.520 --> 01:59:00.580
We obviously go through an entire
quantitative process before we actually
arrive at this stage at the qualitative
analysis.

01:59:01.440 --> 01:59:12.940
And by simply going through the
quantitative process, we are obviously
going to pick up little tidbits of
qualitative information as we go through
that process.

01:59:13.140 --> 01:59:15.060
It's kind of unavoidable.

01:59:15.060 --> 01:59:18.940
We are going to get some information
that's qualitative along the way.

01:59:19.500 --> 01:59:34.800
The key here is understanding the
difference between a quantitative metric
like revenue, earnings, and anything
that's a measure on financial statements,
and a qualitative metric.

01:59:35.400 --> 01:59:41.100
So an underlying business driver metric or
measure, okay?

01:59:41.920 --> 01:59:51.560
And when you actually understand that and
understand the difference and split them
out properly, your process just becomes
more systematic.

01:59:52.100 --> 01:59:54.520
So with KPIs, what are we doing?

01:59:55.000 --> 01:59:57.060
We are looking under the hood.

01:59:57.320 --> 01:59:59.420
We're looking under the bonnet of the car.

01:59:59.420 --> 02:00:13.300
And what we have to remember all the time
is that KPIs are an inference of the
management operating plan, and the
management operating plan drives future
revenue and earnings.

02:00:13.300 --> 02:00:35.320
So we go through this KPI identification
process so we can put those metrics into
the context of future guidance of revenue
and earnings, and then we gauge whether
there's going to be an upwards or
downwards revision of guidance for revenue
and earnings.

02:00:35.580 --> 02:00:42.220
Now, the investor relations website,
that's not the only place where we can
find KPIs.

02:00:42.660 --> 02:01:01.460
When you go through the identification
process, you can take an extra step and go
to the earnings call transcripts to fill
in any missing blanks, any missing
information that you may have overlooked
or may not be included on the investor
relations website.

02:01:01.460 --> 02:01:14.080
So we can go to past earnings call
transcripts, go through them and look for
all the clues and pull out in all the
information and infer a management
operating plan from those KPIs.

02:01:14.660 --> 02:01:20.380
Now, of course, earnings releases and
earnings call transcripts going forward.

02:01:20.540 --> 02:01:29.140
They're very important for us as traders
and you're going to you're going to you're
going to have positions over earnings in
the future.

02:01:30.140 --> 02:01:41.740
And you definitely want to be on top of
earnings when they come out and be going
through them and also going through the
earnings call and the call transcripts.

02:01:42.420 --> 02:01:45.960
Very, very, very important because you're
going to have positions over earnings.

02:01:46.580 --> 02:02:00.440
So, of course, you need to get used to
this process of looking at earnings
information and earnings call transcripts
because that gives you practice to what's
coming up in the future and it makes you
better at processing them.

02:02:00.700 --> 02:02:06.220
So, if you have a current position going
into an earnings day, what's going to
happen?

02:02:06.220 --> 02:02:15.940
The earnings and the earnings call
transcript information is just going to
confirm or deny your fundamental work and
hence your current position.

02:02:16.220 --> 02:02:26.020
You're going to have to make decisions on
the position based on this information,
based on revenue, earnings and even the
information from KPIs.

02:02:26.480 --> 02:02:32.740
OK, and you're either going to run the
position, you're going to add to it or
you're going to trade out of the position.

02:02:32.920 --> 02:02:47.840
Now, if you've got no position going into
earnings, the information on the financial
metrics, so revenue, earnings and
additional financial measures, plus any
discussion about the KPIs that moves the
needle of the KPIs.

02:02:48.820 --> 02:02:52.520
If you have no position going into
earnings, what's going to happen?

02:02:52.980 --> 02:02:58.860
You're going to decide whether you're
going to have a position or not after
earnings based on that information.

02:02:58.860 --> 02:03:10.920
So, it's very important to practice this
stuff and just going through the process,
the qualitative process, you'll be better
at identifying KPIs and whether the needle
is going to move.

02:03:11.720 --> 02:03:19.280
Also, at the qualitative phase, we looked
at management track record, the board of
directors, the quality of the board.

02:03:19.860 --> 02:03:28.600
We looked at insider stock and stock
option ownership, and we looked at
research analyst estimates, range and
ratings.

02:03:28.600 --> 02:03:43.780
And for the qualitative info for
management track record, board of
directors, stock ownership and stock
option ownership, what position you're
intending to have is very important.

02:03:44.120 --> 02:03:50.360
So, what lens you're looking through when
you're assessing this information, this
qualitative information.

02:03:50.900 --> 02:03:58.720
So, for example, if you're potentially
looking to go long, what is your primary
concern here?

02:03:58.720 --> 02:04:07.920
Your primary concern is you want to make
sure that there's no red flags in any of
this qualitative information.

02:04:08.520 --> 02:04:19.920
So, red flags from management track
record, red flags from the board of
directors, and also the stock ownership,
option ownership, and recent trading in
the stock and options.

02:04:19.920 --> 02:04:31.660
If you're intending to go long and you
start uncovering red flags, you obviously
have to do a bit more further work, do
more of a deep dive on that information.

02:04:32.340 --> 02:04:38.280
And if that information ends up putting
you off being long, then that's totally
fine.

02:04:38.520 --> 02:04:40.860
So, just go through that process.

02:04:41.460 --> 02:04:52.060
If everything agrees, however, that
everything's okay, there's no red flags,
then it's probably going to be a situation
where you move on to the next stage of the
process.

02:04:52.680 --> 02:04:55.040
On the short side, what are we looking
for?

02:04:55.120 --> 02:04:56.060
What are we concerned with?

02:04:56.480 --> 02:05:05.620
Well, we actually, if we're looking to go
short a stock, we look at all of this
information in the qualitative phase, and
we uncover red flags.

02:05:05.920 --> 02:05:07.080
That's great for us.

02:05:07.080 --> 02:05:10.740
But, if there's no red flags, it might
make you think twice.

02:05:11.100 --> 02:05:12.080
It's all situational.

02:05:12.240 --> 02:05:14.460
You just have to go through it and figure
it out.

02:05:15.120 --> 02:05:21.480
For research analysts, the estimates, the
range of estimates, and research analyst
ratings,

02:05:22.080 --> 02:05:31.460
because we know that research analysts are
essentially career-driven, risk-averse,
political animals,

02:05:31.980 --> 02:05:41.120
because we know that, most of the time,
their ratings, their price targets, they
are lagging the stock price.

02:05:41.120 --> 02:05:52.160
So, ideally, what we want to see when we
look at this information, we want to see
that there's room for longs, for example.

02:05:52.540 --> 02:05:54.140
We want to see room for upside.

02:05:54.720 --> 02:05:57.780
And for shorts, we want to see that
there's room for downside.

02:05:58.240 --> 02:06:02.720
So, for longs, we want to see that there's
room for upside, so upgrades.

02:06:03.060 --> 02:06:16.260
So, when we go through this overall
process, guys, the qualitative process,
what we're looking for,

02:06:16.260 --> 02:06:25.860
what we want to see is evidence that the
qualitative assessment supports our
fundamental quantitative work, okay?

02:06:26.280 --> 02:06:31.100
That's what we're looking for when we're
going through this whole process, okay?

02:06:31.540 --> 02:06:39.200
The information supporting on the
qualitative side, the quantitative work
that we've already done, okay?

02:06:39.740 --> 02:06:47.020
So, go through the qualitative process, in
addition to the quant process, it's very
important that you do that.

02:06:47.860 --> 02:06:53.700
Now, we're going to move on to the next
stage of the PTM video series,

02:06:53.820 --> 02:06:56.860
the next stage of the trade idea
generation process.

02:06:57.300 --> 02:06:59.220
It's a very, very important part.

